💡 **SUGGESTION: unused parameter `owner` in `_lazyProbeGuardsFail`**

The new composite receives `owner` but never reads it — every guard it
composes uses only `q`, `item`, `provider`, `machineAuthor` and `repoInfo`:

```js
function _lazyProbeGuardsFail(q, item, provider, machineAuthor, owner, repoInfo) {
    if (q.mergeStateLatch && _mergeStateLatchGuardFail(q, item, repoInfo)) return true;
    if (_reviewStaleGuardFail(q, item, provider)) return true;
    if (_staleVerdictGuardFail(q, item, provider)) return true;
    if (_verdictRecordGuardsFail(q, item, provider, machineAuthor)) return true;
    return _threadsGuardsFail(q, item, provider);
}
```

Drop the parameter (and its argument at the `matchesGuards` call site): the
two composites are already asymmetric — `_stateGuardsFail` takes no
`provider` — so signature symmetry does not justify the dead slot. A dead
parameter invites a future caller to pass the wrong thing unnoticed.
No behaviour impact either way.

💡 **SUGGESTION: `isRawLatchMap` and `isLatchEntryObject` are byte-identical**

Two of the new `normalizeMap` helpers are the same expression under
different names:

```js
function isRawLatchMap(raw) {
    return !!raw && typeof raw === 'object' && !Array.isArray(raw);
}

function isLatchEntryObject(entry) {
    return !!entry && typeof entry === 'object' && !Array.isArray(entry);
}
```

Keeping the two names is defensible if they are deliberate
intent-documentation (latch-map shape vs latch-entry shape). If not, a
single shared predicate (`isPlainObject(value)`, used at both call sites —
or one named helper plus a thin alias) avoids shipping the same logic
twice and keeps a future tweak (e.g. rejecting non-plain prototypes) in
one place. Optional polish — no behaviour impact.

